# ================================================================ letter texts
@lettertext 1
Çeşme, 3 March 1971

Yusuf,

I have started this letter eleven times and torn up ten, so you will forgive the handwriting, which was never good and is worse angry. I am not angry now. I was angry for three years and it has worn through like a sole.

The boat was Father's and he left it to both of us and I took it to the mainland as if it were mine. You said I would. You were right and I hated you for it, which is how I know you were right.

Here is the thing. There is a concession at Ilıca, two boats' worth, and the man who held it has died with no sons. I have the money for half. I do not have a brother for the other half unless you come. There is a berth, a house behind the fish hall with two rooms and a fig tree, and a town that does not go dark when the ferry stops.

Come, Yusuf. Bring Mother if she will come and bring the nets if she will not. I will not ask again, not because I am proud but because I do not think I can stand to write this twelfth time.

Your brother,
Kemal
@endletter

@lettertext 2
İstanbul Municipal Conservatory
Department of Voice

14 July 1978

Dear Miss Feride,

The jury of the entrance examination held on 2 June has the pleasure of informing you that you have been admitted to the Department of Voice, soprano, with a full scholarship under the provincial talent provision, covering tuition, lodging in the women's residence, and a monthly stipend.

Instruction begins on 2 October. Candidates are asked to present themselves at the Secretariat on 30 September with this letter, two photographs and their identity papers.

The jury wishes to add, outside the formal record, that the folk setting you offered as your second piece was performed from memory, without accompaniment, and that two members of the jury have asked whether it has been written down anywhere. If it has not, we would ask you to bring it with you.

With our congratulations,

The Secretary of the Jury
@endletter

@lettertext 3
Republic of Türkiye
Directorate of Coastal Safety
Lighthouse Service

Ref. 1989/3310 · 21 November 1989

Keeper Halil,

Your petition of 8 September, requesting transfer from Kuzgun Light to a mainland station on compassionate grounds following the death of your wife, has been reviewed and approved.

You are offered the post of keeper at Kuşadası South Light, effective 1 February 1990, with the service house attached (three rooms, mains electricity, a school within two kilometres). Kuzgun Light will pass to the automatic programme in the following year.

Please confirm acceptance in writing by 31 December 1989. Failing a reply, the offer will be understood as declined and your name removed from the transfer list for the present cycle.

The Directorate extends its condolences.

For the Director,
Chief of the Lighthouse Service
@endletter

@lettertext 4
Kuzgun, 11 August 1986

Hasan,

I am writing this on the back counter at eleven at night, which is when the post office tells the truth, and I am going to put it in the sack for the morning and you will have it in your hands on your own boat, where you cannot walk away from me, and that is the only way I can see to do this.

I have loved you since the summer of 1962, when you were the mate and the Kuzgun I had the engine fire and you carried the post sack off the boat before you carried your own bag, and you put it on my counter and said, there, nothing lost, and I thought, that is the kind of man.

I have not said it in twenty-four years because a woman who keeps the post cannot be seen waiting for a ferry. Everyone comes to the counter. Everyone would know. And I had been left on a pier once and I decided I would be the one on the land after that, and never the one on the water looking back.

I am fifty-three. You are fifty-three. We have wasted an amount of time that I do not propose to count.

If you read this and come up the steps, I will put the kettle on. If you read this and do not, I will still come to meet every boat, and you will never know whether it is the post I am waiting for, and I will let you not know. It is the kindest thing I can think of.

Nermin
@endletter

@lettertext 5
Egeport Tourism and Investment A.Ş.
Alsancak, İzmir

19 May 1992

To the Headman of Kuzgun Village,

Further to our representative's visit of April, we set out below the terms on which Egeport proposes to acquire the parcel known as the Cove (cadastral lots 114 to 121), with foreshore rights, for the development of a boutique hotel of forty rooms.

Purchase price: as discussed, payable one third on signature and the balance on licensing. Employment: not fewer than twenty positions, preference to residents of the village. Transport: Egeport will operate a private passenger launch between the Cove and Çeşme, twice daily in season and three times weekly otherwise, with places for residents at the subsidised fare, for the lifetime of the hotel.

We draw your attention to the last point. Our understanding is that the state subsidy for the public ferry is not secure. A private service tied to the hotel would be.

Access to the Cove beach would be reserved for the hotel's guests. This condition is not negotiable.

We would ask for the village council's reply within the year.

Yours faithfully,
Director of Development
@endletter

@lettertext 6
Hamburg, 20 November 1983

Deniz,

You are eight and you will not read this for some years, if your grandmother lets you read it at all. I am writing it anyway because I want it to exist in a drawer somewhere with my handwriting on it, so that when you ask, someone can hand it to you instead of telling you a story.

I am not coming back to the island. I want to say that plainly because everyone there will say it for me in worse ways. I tried for two years to want to come back and I could not make myself want it, and your mother was right to stop waiting.

I am not leaving you. I know that is a thing people say. Here is what I mean by it. I am at the address at the top of this page, and I will be at that address or I will write to the post office with the next one, every time, for as long as I am alive. You do not have to write. You do not have to come. You only have to know that there is an end of the line on this side that is not going anywhere.

I did not say goodbye to her properly. If she is reading this over your shoulder, tell her I know.

Your father,
Tarık
@endletter

@lettertext 7
Kuzgun, April 1994

For whoever opens this drawer.

If it is a man from the directorate, put them back and lock it and I will haunt you. If it is Deniz, sit down.

There are six letters under this one. I did not deliver them. I read every one, which is a crime, and I kept every one, which is a worse one, and I have no defence except the one I am about to give, which is not a defence.

In 1958 a man I had married got on the ten o'clock and did not write. I stood on that pier a long time. When I came back to the counter I found I could not put a letter in someone's hand if I knew it would take them to the water. I told myself it was for the island. The island was a hundred and ninety people then. It is a hundred and forty now, so you may judge how well that worked.

Each one of these I meant to deliver the next week. The next week became the drawer.

I am not asking you to forgive me, child; that is between me and whoever keeps the counter after this. I am asking you something smaller and harder. Do not burn them. I could not deliver them. You can. Go and put them in people's hands and stand there while they read, which is the part I could never do. Some of them will hate me and one or two will hate you and the island will talk, and it will be the first honest week it has had since I took the keys.

Yours is in there. I am sorry for that one most. I read it too.

Nermin
@endletter

