# ================================================================ letter 6: Deniz
@scene deliver6 bg=postoffice time=noon lut=sepia music=drawer ambience=clock
@if drawer_action == 1 -> d6_read
Yours. Hamburg, 1983. You take it out of the drawer and put it in your inside pocket, unopened,
  where you can feel the corner of it against your ribs when you breathe.
Not yet. Perhaps not at all. It has waited eleven years with its mouth shut and it can wait for
  you to decide whether you want to hear it.
@flag kept_six
@set aux0 =0
@jump afternoon_choice

@label d6_read
@set aux0 =6
@unflag kept_six
You sit in her chair. You use her letter knife, because it is there. The paper has been folded so
  long that it has to be persuaded flat.
@letter 6
@flag read.6
You read it once fast and once slowly and then you sit with it on your knee and the clock going.
Eight. You were eight. You had a father who had an address.
Deniz:
  > "He's had eleven years to write again. He didn't." {hard} -> d6_hard
  > "He said he would write the post office with each new address. She'd have had those too." {write} -> d6_honest
  > "Put it away. Not today." {shut} -> d6_shut

@label d6_hard
You fold it. You feel, mostly, that you would like to be angry, and that you cannot find where to
  put it, because the two people you would be angry at are in Hamburg and in the ground.
@set resolve +0
@jump d6_end

@label d6_honest
You look. The front two drawers, the ledger, the backs of the pigeonholes. Nothing. Then, in the
  lining of her coat, folded very small, four envelopes with German stamps, addressed to the post
  office, each one with the same two lines in it and a different street.
The last is dated this January. He is alive. He has moved four times. He is at the end of a line
  that is not going anywhere.
@set resolve +1
@set aux4 =1
@jump d6_end

@label d6_shut
You put it back in the drawer, open now, on top of the others. It is a different thing from
  before. It cannot be unread. But it can be unanswered, for today.
@jump d6_end

@label d6_end
@if aux4 == 1 -> d6_radio_hint
@jump afternoon_choice
@label d6_radio_hint
There is no telephone on the island that reaches Germany. There is a radio room at the top of the
  lighthouse, and a keeper's daughter who knows how to work it.
@jump afternoon_choice
